{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/2026-07-07-japanese-culture-habits-that-can-feel-different/audio/main-3742a765cf08.mp3","durationSec":350.76,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.58,"text":"In Japan, silence is not always awkward."},{"start":3.34,"end":8.12,"text":"A quiet moment can show respect, careful thinking, or the wish not to speak too directly."},{"start":9.1,"end":12.7,"text":"In many Western settings, people often fill silence with conversation."},{"start":13.5,"end":15.72,"text":"If nobody speaks, it can feel uncomfortable."},{"start":16.8,"end":20.38,"text":"In a Japanese conversation, a pause can give people time."},{"start":21.2,"end":24.32,"text":"It can also protect the relationship when the topic is sensitive."},{"start":25.14,"end":27.6,"text":"This does not mean every quiet person agrees."},{"start":28.42,"end":31.16,"text":"It means silence can carry social meaning."},{"start":32.52,"end":38.06,"text":"In Japan, people often avoid a strong direct \"no,\" especially in formal or delicate situations."},{"start":38.82,"end":43.26,"text":"They may say \"That might be difficult,\" \"I'll think about it,\" or \"Maybe another time.\""},{"start":44.2,"end":47.6,"text":"The answer can sound soft, but the meaning may still be no."},{"start":48.3,"end":50.96,"text":"This style can feel polite because it lowers pressure."},{"start":51.54,"end":54.58,"text":"It can also confuse visitors who expect a direct answer."},{"start":54.58,"end":60,"text":"A useful listening question is: Is this person really agreeing, or are they gently refusing?"},{"start":61.46,"end":63.52,"text":"- In Japan, silence can show..."},{"start":63.52,"end":65.98,"text":"- A soft answer may mean..."},{"start":66.52,"end":68.26,"text":"- This can feel different because..."},{"start":68.26,"end":73.52,"text":"In Japan, people usually take off outdoor shoes before entering a home."},{"start":74.22,"end":79.2,"text":"Some traditional inns, temples, hot springs, and restaurants also have no-shoe areas."},{"start":80.02,"end":83.48,"text":"This separates the outside ground from the clean inside space."},{"start":84.02,"end":88.9,"text":"For Japanese people, wearing outdoor shoes in a home can feel dirty or uncomfortable."},{"start":90.06,"end":94.08,"text":"For visitors, the important skill is simple: look at the entrance."},{"start":95.04,"end":100.58,"text":"If shoes are lined up, slippers are waiting, or the floor rises, stop and check."},{"start":101.8,"end":108.36,"text":"When Japanese people travel, they often buy omiyage, or small gifts, for family, friends, or coworkers."},{"start":108.36,"end":111.62,"text":"Omiyage is not only a memory of the trip."},{"start":111.62,"end":115.32,"text":"It can also show thanks and show that you thought about other people."},{"start":116.8,"end":123.26,"text":"Many omiyage gifts are local sweets or snacks, often packed in small pieces so they are easy to share."},{"start":124.14,"end":132.08,"text":"In many Western settings, people buy souvenirs for themselves or close family, but they may not bring gifts for everyone at work after a short trip."},{"start":133.24,"end":134.92,"text":"- People take off shoes because..."},{"start":134.92,"end":138.54,"text":"- Omiyage is more than a souvenir because..."},{"start":138.54,"end":141.58,"text":"- This habit shows care for..."},{"start":143.02,"end":147.4,"text":"Japanese customer service is often careful and formal."},{"start":147.4,"end":153.14,"text":"Staff may bow, use very polite language, and try to make the customer feel comfortable."},{"start":154.34,"end":159.34,"text":"This is connected with omotenashi, a Japanese idea of wholehearted hospitality."},{"start":160.46,"end":164.76,"text":"In many Western settings, service can be friendly but more casual."},{"start":164.76,"end":168.52,"text":"Staff may speak directly or joke with customers."},{"start":169.08,"end":173.6,"text":"Japanese service can feel beautiful, but some visitors may also feel it is very formal."},{"start":174.32,"end":177,"text":"Both styles can be good; they just create a different feeling."},{"start":178.12,"end":181.56,"text":"In Japan, people often think about the group before speaking or acting."},{"start":182.18,"end":186.34,"text":"At school, work, or in the community, people may try not to disturb others."},{"start":187.1,"end":190.24,"text":"This idea is connected with wa, or social harmony."},{"start":191.6,"end":194.58,"text":"Group harmony does not mean Japanese people have no personal opinions."},{"start":195.26,"end":199.76,"text":"It means people may choose the right time, place, and words to express them."},{"start":199.76,"end":206.02,"text":"A strong opinion may be easier to share after listening, reading the room, and keeping the relationship smooth."},{"start":207.48,"end":208.88,"text":"- Japanese service can feel..."},{"start":210.42,"end":211.68,"text":"- Group harmony means..."},{"start":211.68,"end":214.78,"text":"- In my country, people usually..."},{"start":216.4,"end":219.14,"text":"In Japan, trains and buses are often quiet."},{"start":219.9,"end":222.54,"text":"Talking loudly on the phone is usually seen as bad manners."},{"start":223.02,"end":228.66,"text":"People may still talk softly with friends, but the public space is shared, so voices are often lower."},{"start":229.47,"end":232.58,"text":"In some Western countries, public transport can be louder."},{"start":233.3,"end":237.02,"text":"People may chat, take calls, or act more openly."},{"start":237.58,"end":244.08,"text":"A visitor in Japan does not need to be perfect, but noticing the volume around you is a useful sign of respect."},{"start":245.16,"end":247.78,"text":"Japan often connects seasons with daily life."},{"start":247.78,"end":257.06,"text":"Cherry blossoms in spring, summer festivals, red leaves in autumn, and hot foods in winter can all shape how people enjoy the year."},{"start":257.96,"end":260.6,"text":"Seasonal culture also appears in shops."},{"start":261.58,"end":267.46,"text":"Snacks, drinks, packages, clothes, and decorations may change for a short time only."},{"start":268.38,"end":275.14,"text":"Many countries enjoy seasons, but Japan often makes the seasonal feeling visible in many small everyday things."},{"start":276.24,"end":283.22,"text":"In Japan, people may say sorry to keep a relationship smooth, even when the problem is not fully their fault."},{"start":283.8,"end":292.3,"text":"The apology can mean \"I care about the trouble\" or \"I want to repair the situation,\" not only \"I did something bad.\""},{"start":292.74,"end":297.86,"text":"In some Western settings, people may avoid saying sorry if they do not want to accept blame."},{"start":298.48,"end":299.88,"text":"This can create misunderstanding."},{"start":300.84,"end":305.6,"text":"In Japan, an apology can be a social tool for repair, trust, and care."},{"start":306.5,"end":314.48,"text":"In Japan, small details often carry meaning: clean presentation, careful timing, polite wording, and beautiful wrapping."},{"start":315.24,"end":317.28,"text":"A simple snack may be wrapped neatly."},{"start":317.7,"end":319.62,"text":"A shop may prepare the bag carefully."},{"start":320.32,"end":323.98,"text":"A train station may use clear lines and signs to guide people."},{"start":325.02,"end":331.04,"text":"This attention to detail is one reason many Japanese products and services feel high quality."},{"start":331.46,"end":334.34,"text":"But detail also takes time and effort."},{"start":334.98,"end":338.54,"text":"A useful question is not \"Which culture is better?\""},{"start":338.96,"end":341.58,"text":"It is \"What does this culture try to protect?\""},{"start":342.8,"end":344.22,"text":"- This habit helps protect..."},{"start":345.28,"end":347.48,"text":"- In my country, people usually..."},{"start":348.6,"end":350.28,"text":"- I prefer this style when..."}],"words":[{"text":"In","start":0,"end":0.18},{"text":"J